    <!-- Fork (白い熊 考直): Diagnostics (KojikiDiag) — every switch off by default; off costs nothing. -->
    <string name="kojiki_diag_section">Diagnostics</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_intro">Each switch records one thing to its own trace, all off by default. Off means nothing is recorded and nothing runs. Traces are capped at 512 KiB and also readable with adb under Android/data/%1$s/files/diag/, the app’s own data directory.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_sub_app">App</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_sub_net">Tunnel &amp; network</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_leaks">Screen leaks</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_leaks_desc">15 s after a screen closes, forces a garbage collection and records every closed screen still held in memory.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_heap">Heap dump on next leak</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_heap_desc">One-shot: the next leak found also writes heap.hprof (~60 MB) beside the trace, then this switch turns itself off. Needs Screen leaks on.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_vpn">VPN restarts</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_vpn_desc">Every process start, boot broadcast and VPN start attempt, with WireGuard’s state after a start; every engine start, recycle and stop with its reason; network changes — a loss held for 15 s, a network that came back in time, and, when no network is usable, what each one lacked; and how many connections still aim at stand-in (ALG) addresses from before a restart: counted 60 s and 5 min after each start, the first five named.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_dns">DNS watchdog</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_dns_desc">Every failed DNS query, and each recycle or failover the watchdog performs.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_wg">WireGuard health</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_wg_desc">Each minute, per active tunnel: status, handshake age, bytes and the last error — written when something changes or a handshake is over 3 min old, plus a line every 15 min.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_threads">Thread dumps</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_threads_desc">When kojiki stalls — DNS questions unanswered for 30 s, or the service still running 5 s after STOP — writes every thread’s stack, so the trace shows what is holding it. At most one a minute.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_engine">Engine log</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_engine_desc">The network engine’s own lines about name resolution, address translation and the resolver’s connections. While on, the engine runs at its verbose log level, which costs some speed — switch it on only to catch something.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_goroutines_now">Dump engine threads now</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_goroutines_now_desc">Writes the stack of every thread inside the engine to the engine log, whether the switch is on or not.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_probe">Translation probe (2 min)</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_probe_desc">For two minutes: address translation on “auto”, the engine log captured, and the engine’s translation threads sampled every 4 s. Reproduces the lookup hang on hosts without an IPv6 address; lookups of such hosts can stall while it runs. Ends by itself.</string>
    <string name="kojiki_diag_probe_started">Translation probe running for 2 minutes.</string>
